ife: Household Energy and Health. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2006. </citation></ref><ref id="CR20"><label>20.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>KR</given-names></name><name><surname>Mehta</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Feuz</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Ezzati</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Indoor air pollution from household use of solid fuels</article-title><source>Comparative Quantification of Health Risks: Global and Regional Burden of Disease Attributable to Selected Major Risk Factors</source><year>2004</year><publisher-loc>Geneva</publisher-loc><publisher-name>World Health Organization</publisher-name></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Smith KR, Mehta S, Feuz M. Indoor air pollution from household use of solid fuels. In: Ezzati M et al., eds. Comparative Quantification of Health Risks: Global and Regional Burden of Disease Attributable to Selected Major Risk Factors. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2004. </citation></ref><ref id="CR21"><label>21.</label><citation citation-type="book"><source>Air Quality Guidelines for Particulate Matter, Ozone, Nitrogen Dioxide and Sulfur Dioxide&#x02014;Global Update 2005</source><year>2006</year><publisher-loc>Geneva</publisher-loc><publisher-name>World Health Organization</publisher-name></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">WHO. Air Quality Guidelines for Particulate Matter, Ozone, Nitrogen Dioxide and Sulfur Dioxide&#x02014;Global Update 2005. Geneva: World Health Organization; 2006. </citation></ref><ref id="CR22"><label>22.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Levy</surname><given-names>BS</given-names></name><name><surname>Wegman</surname><given-names>DH</